import{_ as s,o as n,c as a,a2 as t}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Development environment","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"dev/environment.md","filePath":"dev/environment.md"}'),o={name:"dev/environment.md"};function i(l,e,r,c,p,d){return n(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t(`<h1 id="development-environment" tabindex="-1">Development environment <a class="header-anchor" href="#development-environment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Development environment&quot;">​</a></h1><p>How to go from a fresh machine to hacking on the kernel - the toolchain contract, the two dependency gates, the no-root sandbox install, and the layout you will be navigating.</p><h2 id="_1-requirements" tabindex="-1">1. Requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A Linux host (Debian/Ubuntu tested; anything with the packages below works). Windows: WSL2 gives you exactly this environment (<code>build.ps1</code> dispatches to it; MSYS2 lacks xorriso/grub).</li><li><strong>The release toolchain</strong>: gcc-14 (Debian gcc 14.2.0 is the compiler the releases are built with; CI prefers <code>gcc-14</code> with a <code>gcc</code> fallback), binutils <code>ld</code>, <strong>nasm 2.16.03</strong>, xorriso <strong>1.5.6</strong>, mtools, grub-common + grub-pc-bin + grub-efi-amd64-bin, python3 (3.9+), zip.</li><li>For testing: <strong>qemu-system-x86 10.0.13</strong>, <strong>OVMF</strong>, seabios, ipxe-qemu.</li><li>For the E2E suites: Python <code>pexpect</code> (regression, mega-app smoke) and <code>paramiko</code> (SSH E2E).</li><li>Cross bring-up shells only: <code>gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu</code>, <code>gcc-riscv64-linux-gnu</code> (GCC &gt;= 12).</li></ul><p>Two different &quot;check_deps&quot; exist - do not confuse them:</p><ol><li><strong>Host-package check</strong> - <code>tools/build.sh</code> verifies the build set (gcc, ld/binutils, nasm, grub-mkimage/grub-common, xorriso, mformat+mcopy/mtools, python3, zip), printing the exact Debian package per command; <code>--install</code> apt-installs them as root, otherwise it points at the no-root alternative below.</li><li><strong>Architecture gate</strong> - <code>tools/check_deps.py</code> enforces the <em>dependency direction</em> over <code>ocos/{kernel,drivers,services,l1apps}</code>: <ul><li>kernel must not include anything resolving under <code>drivers/</code> or <code>services/</code> (exception: the <code>core_boot_*.c</code> composition root),</li><li>drivers must not include services,</li><li>l1apps may only include <code>ocos/interface/</code>-owned headers,</li><li>plain-basename <code>#include &quot;x.h&quot;</code> resolution against a real file inventory; any violation exits 1 with file:line, the include, and the resolved owner. Run it before release hygiene (it is not wired into CI).</li></ul></li></ol><h2 id="_2-the-no-root-sandbox-install" tabindex="-1">2. The no-root sandbox install <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-the-no-root-sandbox-install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. The no-root sandbox install&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>tools/install-toolchain.sh</code> downloads Debian <code>.deb</code>s and extracts them with <code>dpkg -x</code> into <code>$OC_OPT</code> (default <code>~/opt/extract</code>) - ~117 packages: nasm 2.16.03, qemu-system-x86 10.0.13 (+ data + libs), xorriso 1.5.6, grub-pc-bin + grub-efi-amd64-bin + grub-common, mtools, ovmf, seabios. gcc itself comes from the system. Then:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>source tools/env-sandbox.sh   # exports OC_TOOLS, PATH, LD_LIBRARY_PATH</span></span></code></pre></div><p>or point the Makefile at the tree directly: <code>make OC_TOOLS=&lt;tree&gt; ...</code> - it probes <code>&lt;tree&gt;/usr/...</code> and <code>&lt;tree&gt;/...</code> layouts; <code>OVMF_FD</code>/<code>QEMU_DATADIR</code>/<code>SEABIOS_DIR</code> derive from <code>OC_TOOLS</code> and can be overridden individually.</p><h2 id="_3-getting-the-tree-and-the-sanity-build" tabindex="-1">3. Getting the tree and the sanity build <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-getting-the-tree-and-the-sanity-build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Getting the tree and the sanity build&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>git clone https://github.com/cubestudio-dev/OpenCubeOS.git</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>cd OpenCubeOS</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>make all             # userprogs + kernel + ISO + src zip + OTA pkg</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>make test            # host unit tests</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>make run-bios        # boot it</span></span></code></pre></div><p>If all three pass, your environment is good. Note CI clones with <code>fetch-depth: 0</code> - the build id is <code>git rev-list --count HEAD + 21</code>, and a shallow clone would bake a build id below the published ones (every installed machine would ignore the release as a &quot;downgrade&quot;). The full bar for a change is in <a href="./../root/CONTRIBUTING.html">CONTRIBUTING.md</a>.</p><h2 id="_4-repository-layout" tabindex="-1">4. Repository layout <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-repository-layout" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Repository layout&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>ocos/kernel/      L0 core: boot chain, sched, PMM/VMM/heap, usermode</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocos/kernel/arch/x86_64/  the 64-bit arch layer (cross archs in build.mk)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocos/services/    one folder per service facet: block, net, audio,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                  fs, selfhost (OTA), power, diag, health, klog,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                  watchdog, display, tui, crypto, camera, usb, ...</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocos/drivers/     one folder per class: storage, net, audio, bus</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                  (pci/acpi/i2c), usb, input, display, time, misc</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocos/interface/   the ring-3 contract headers: l1_lib.h,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                  l1svc_ops.h, i18n.h, wizard_lib.h, fs_lib.h,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                  editor_core.h, oc_l1_api.h, crt0.asm</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocos/l1apps/      one command = one app = one folder (284 programs)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                  + _i18n/ for the flat legacy test programs</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocire/            the independent recovery kernel (second L0)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocbl/             the in-tree bootloader (BIOS stage1 + EFI entry)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>ocse/             the vendored install-environment kernel</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tools/            build + test + docs generators (117 files)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>tests/            host-side unit tests (make test)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>docs/             this documentation tree (en + zh mirror)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>etc/              the default /etc volume content + l0 i18n</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="_5-conventions-that-matter-immediately" tabindex="-1">5. Conventions that matter immediately <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-conventions-that-matter-immediately" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Conventions that matter immediately&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>-Wall -Wextra -Werror</code> - zero warnings, no exceptions, on every component.</li><li>Every file carries the SPDX header.</li><li>Kernel/service-visible strings are ASCII English (they are the script + wizard-anchor contract); human surfaces render through <code>tr_()</code> with en+zh resources.</li><li>The command surface only grows (interface freeze; the numbering is append-only - see <a href="./interface-dictionary.html">interface-dictionary.md</a>).</li><li>Big buffers live on the heap (kernel) or static BSS (ring 3) - the kernel image is pinned below 0x400000 (see <a href="./build.html">build.md</a> section 4).</li></ul><h2 id="_6-qemu-recipes" tabindex="-1">6. QEMU recipes <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-qemu-recipes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. QEMU recipes&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span># quick BIOS boot, serial on stdio</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512M -cdrom build/opencube.iso \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    -netdev user,id=n1 -device e1000,netdev=n1 -serial mon:stdio -display none</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span># UEFI (OVMF; the make target copies OVMF.fd to a pflash work copy)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512M -machine q35 \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    -drive if=pflash,format=raw,file=OVMF_WORK.fd \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    -cdrom build/opencube.iso -display none -serial mon:stdio</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span># with a scratch disk + display for the TUI</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512M -cdrom build/opencube.iso \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    -drive file=scratch.qcow2,if=ide -netdev user,id=n1 \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    -device e1000,netdev=n1</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span># boot log to a file</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>... -serial file:boot.log</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>make run-bios</code>, <code>make run-uefi</code>, <code>make run-bios-gui</code>, <code>make run-uefi-gui</code>, <code>make run-bios-persist</code>, <code>make shot-*</code> wrap the common ones (including the fresh 32 MiB <code>/etc</code> volume setup so persistence tests start clean). A relocated toolchain: <code>make OC_TOOLS=&lt;tree&gt; run-bios</code>. Deeper QEMU guidance - errata, sendkey, firmware paths - lives in <a href="./qemu-testing.html">qemu-testing.md</a>.</p><h2 id="_7-host-unit-tests" tabindex="-1">7. Host unit tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-host-unit-tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Host unit tests&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>make test</code> (<code>tools/run_host_tests.py</code>) extracts the gcc line from each <code>tests/host_*.c</code> header, remaps pre-restructure paths, compiles and runs on the host compiler: crypto vectors (NIST AES/SHA/HMAC, RSA, X.509, AEAD), TLS key schedule, TCP congestion control, block-512 gate, multiboot2 parsing, BT/WiFi/camera/SCSI/ IR/touchpad subsystems. Several harnesses use ASAN locally; fixture-dependent ones (openssl RSA/chain fixtures) declare SKIP-FIXTURE when the fixtures are absent. A regression here is much faster to iterate on than a boot loop. Full list: <a href="./host-tests.html">host-tests.md</a>.</p>`,22)])])}const m=s(o,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
